# 50% sampling robustness analysis v1

## Result

The repeated full sparse reference reproduced macro-F1 0.675548 exactly; its
median warm method wall was 2.52240 s.  Peak RSS for the complete follow-up was
298,991,616 bytes.

- Five new uniform-reservoir seeds had median macro-F1 loss 0.031535, loss
  range 0.023025--0.036004, median wall ratio 0.5943, two quality passes, and
  two joint passes.  All samples retained all 20 classes.
- Five stratified-uniform seeds had median loss 0.029314, range
  0.026502--0.031367, median wall ratio 0.5894, three quality passes, and two
  joint passes.  All samples retained all 20 classes.  One otherwise
  quality-passing seed had wall ratio 0.7692 and missed the time gate.

Neither sampler met the prospective aggregate rule requiring median loss at
most 0.03, at least four of five quality passes, median wall ratio at most
0.75, and complete class coverage.  Stratification improved the median but did
not make the tradeoff robust.

## Decision

Close batch example compression on this benchmark.  Together with the prior
pilot, 25% sampling, 50% uniform sampling, 50% stratified sampling, and
SVD-128/256 are all insufficient under their declared gates.  No learned
coreset is justified from these data, and the isolated passing seeds must not
be selected retrospectively.

The next discriminative question is genuinely streaming: can a stateless
hashed representation and one-pass bounded-state classifier approach the full
sparse reference cheaply enough to expose a concrete gap for online moment or
IDF estimation?  This shifts the constraint rather than relabeling batch
subsampling as streaming.

